// Package cairn.worker.v1 defines the contract that every Worker implements.
//
// Workers run as independent processes in any language (Go, Python, Node, ...)
// and connect to the Cairn Core. The Core never invokes worker-specific RPCs
// — all interaction is generic Jobs with provider-specific payloads carried
// in google.protobuf.Any.
//
// Transport (revised): NATS + JetStream is the production transport.
// The message types in this file (Job, JobResult, WorkerManifest, etc.)
// are still the wire format — they're proto-encoded as NATS payloads.
// The Connect/gRPC service definitions below remain available as a
// fallback path for HTTP-only environments and for smoketest debugging
// via curl, but are NOT the primary path. See
// CLAUDE.md§"Multi-Source-Modell" and README.md§"What still needs to be
// written" for the rationale.
//
// NATS subject layout:
//
//   cairn.jobs.<provider>.<job_type>      Core → worker, queue-group balanced
//   cairn.results.<job_id>                worker → Core (or use request-reply)
//   cairn.workers.<name>.<inst>.hb        worker → Core heartbeats
//   cairn.workers.register                request-reply registration
//   cairn.events.<topic>                  internal fan-out
//
// Lifecycle of a worker (NATS-native):
//
//   1. Worker starts, sends a Register request on `cairn.workers.register`
//      (NATS request-reply) carrying its WorkerManifest. The Core replies
//      with the worker_id assigned and the JetStream consumer config the
//      worker should subscribe to.
//
//   2. Worker subscribes to its assigned `cairn.jobs.<provider>.>` subjects
//      with the queue group `cairn-workers-<provider>` (so multiple worker
//      instances of the same provider load-balance).
//
//   3. Worker processes each Job message. Result is published to
//      `cairn.results.<job_id>` OR returned via NATS request-reply
//      depending on whether the caller wanted a synchronous answer.
//      JetStream's ack window (5min default) re-delivers if the worker
//      crashes.
//
//   4. Worker publishes Heartbeat to `cairn.workers.<name>.<inst>.hb`
//      every 15s. A subscriber on the Core consumes these and updates the
//      `workers` table; missing heartbeats for > grace cause OFFLINE
//      marking and JetStream re-delivers any in-flight jobs.
//
//   5. On shutdown, the worker drains JetStream consumers (so jobs in
//      flight finish) and publishes a Deregister event. The Core marks
//      the row deleted; queue-group rebalancing routes pending jobs to
//      other workers for the same provider if available.

